WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.340
The oceans play a huge role

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.380
in really every
elemental cycle.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:06.780
They have a global impact

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.160
on the workings
of the Earth really

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.719
and so we are focusing

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:14.560
on one aspect of that,

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.840
that we know relatively
little about.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.300
How do microbes work together

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.340
to fix carbon,
to transfer carbon

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.750
amongst themselves?

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:22.949
Where does that carbon go?

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:25.230
How quickly does that carbon turnover?

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.410
And so it's important and we'd like to put
all of these data together, think about what

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.870
we already know now about the carbon cycle
and see how this new information might change

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.200
how we think about it, what we predict from
it.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.480
The particular research area that we've chosen
is an area that has been poorly studied in

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:50.290
the past due to analytical challenges that
we feel now are ready to be applied to this

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:51.290
question.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:55.500
It's not just how climate change affects the
microbes, but really then how microbes further

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.060
affect the climate in response to those changes.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.770
Well, one of the things we've already learned
in our workshop so far is bringing together

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:12.080
people of really diverse backgrounds allows
us access to novel perspectives and different

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.740
fields that could be helpful.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:20.000
Certainly we think that a better understanding
of this system will give us a really powerful,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.490
predictive knowledge about what will happen
in a changing climate.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:28.750
So the methods are there now, the interest
is there and I think it wasn't possible 10

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.740
years ago to say this but it is now.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.080
[Music]

